course: dictionary
lesson: 150
title: Affirmation: saying it is true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An affirmation is a firm statement that something is true, or a clear sign that you agree. When Ella tells herself «I am brave» before a test, that is a positive affirmation. The word «positive» is a clue: it means the saying is kind and hopeful.
explain: Affirmation can also mean words that encourage someone. If your teacher says, «You worked hard, and it shows», she is giving you an affirmation of support. A nod of affirmation is a nod that shows someone agrees with you.
explain: «In UK courts people may swear on a holy book or make a non-religious affirmation.» Here the word sits next to swear and holy book, so the clues say it is a promise made in place of a religious oath. Check it: put «promise» into the sentence and it still makes sense.
fact: Affirmation means saying firmly that something is true, or agreeing with it.
fact: Affirmation also means words of support or encouragement that help someone feel good about themselves.
fact: In UK courts, a witness may swear on a holy book or make a non-religious affirmation, which is a solemn promise to tell the truth.
fact: Lying in court is called perjury, and it is a crime.
fact: Phrases to know: positive affirmation, daily affirmations, a nod of affirmation, in affirmation.
exercise: «In UK courts people may swear on a holy book or make a non-religious affirmation.» In this sentence, what is an affirmation? | A holy book | A prayer said out loud in church | *A solemn, non-religious promise to tell the truth | A kind of lie told in court
exercise: Ella says to herself each morning, «I am brave.» What kind of affirmation is this? | A loud shout of anger | *A positive affirmation that lifts you up | A rule about where to sit | A test you must pass
exercise: A nod of affirmation means | A nod to say goodbye | A nod while falling asleep | *A nod that shows you agree | A nod to ask a question
test: Which sentence is an affirmation of support? | *«You can do this, and I believe in you.» | «Lunch is in the hall.» | «Put your coat on before you go out.» | «The bus leaves at nine.»
test: What is lying in court called? | *Perjury, a crime | A positive affirmation | A nod of affirmation | A non-religious promise
exam: «Courts make witnesses swear "the truth, the whole truth and nothing but the truth" because someone's freedom depends on it.» Why do witnesses make this promise? | Because the court wants them to sing a song | Because it is a fun game with no rules | *Because someone's freedom depends on them telling the truth | Because they must always wear a holy book
